prelims.tex:1:In this section we will give the definition of random $k$-CNF Satisfiability (random $k$-CNF SAT) at the threshold. We additionally present definitions of several important distributions and functions that are used later in the paper. %We present theorems and lemmas from previous work we use later in the paper. 
prelims.tex:6:We use $x \sim D$ to indicate that $x$ is a random value drawn from the distribution $D$.
prelims.tex:10:We often use ``small-Hamming-distance assignment'' to mean an assignment that is a small Hamming distance from a satisfying assignment. 
prelims.tex:12:\paragraph{Definitions for This Paper}
prelims.tex:17:	Let $D_{replace}(n, k)$ be the uniform distribution over \textit{clauses} on $k$ variables where those $k$ variables are chosen with \textit{replacement} (e.g. $(\bar{x} \vee x \vee y )$ would be a valid clause). Under this definition, there are $n^k2^k$ possible clauses. 
prelims.tex:21:The density of a formula $\phi$ with $m$ clauses and $n$ variables is $m/n$.
prelims.tex:24:We will now define the \textit{satisfiability threshold}. Informally, this is a density of clauses such that formulas drawn from below this threshold are with high probability (whp) satisfied, and those formulas drawn from above the threshold are whp unsatisfied. 
prelims.tex:27:The \textit{satisfiability threshold}, $d_k$, is a ratio of clauses to variables such that for all $\eps>0$:
prelims.tex:29:	\item If $m$ is drawn from $Pois[(d_k-\eps)n]$, the Poisson distribution with mean $(d_k-\eps)n$, and $\phi$ is formed by picking $m$ clauses independently at random from $D_{replace}(n, k)$, then $\phi$ is whp \textbf{satisfied}. 
prelims.tex:30:	\item If $m$ is drawn from $Pois[(d_k+\eps)n]$, the Poisson distribution with mean $(d_k+\eps)n$, and $\phi$ is formed by picking $m$ clauses independently at random from $D_{replace}(n, k)$, then $\phi$ is whp \textbf{unsatisfied}. 
prelims.tex:35:	Let $d_k$ be the density of clauses such that SAT is at its threshold.
prelims.tex:38:Note that it is not immediate that a satisfiability threshold exists for any given $k$. 
prelims.tex:39:However, Jian Ding, Allan Sly, and Nike Sun showed that this threshold exists for sufficiently large $k$~\cite{threasholdSAT}. It has been proven that 
prelims.tex:40:$$ 2^k \ln(2) - \frac{1}{2}(1+\ln(2)) -\epsilon_k \leq d_k \leq 2^k \ln(2)  - \frac{1}{2}(1+\ln(2)) +\epsilon_k ,$$ 
prelims.tex:41:where $\epsilon_k$ is a term that tends to $0$ as $k$ grows \cite{boundDensitySAT,boundingSATDensity2}. It follows that there exists a large enough $k$ such that $ 2^k \ln(2) - 1 \leq d_k$. Also, there exists a large enough $k$ such that $ 2^k \ln(2) \geq d_k$.
prelims.tex:43:This density determines the distribution over the number of clauses put in the formula. Specifically, $m$ is drawn from $Pois[d_kn]$. However, we can say that with high probability the number of clauses is nearly $d_kn$ (see Lem.~\ref{lem:boundingM}).
prelims.tex:62:Our choice of $\kval$ in the above is somewhat arbitrary. When $k\geq \kval$ the proofs in section \ref{sec:lotsOfSAT} are simpler, so we analyze our core algorithm in that regime. 
prelims.tex:80:It follows that if our algorithm works efficiently for all values of $m \in [(d_k-1)n, (d_k+1)n]$, then it works with high probability at the threshold.
prelims.tex:88:%	Let $D_{unique}(k)$ be the uniform distribution over \textit{clauses} on $k$ variables where those $k$ variables are chosen with \textit{without replacement} (e.g. $(\bar{z} \vee x \vee y )$ is a valid clause). There are $n(n-1)(n-2)\ldots(n-k+1)2^k$ such clauses. 
prelims.tex:92:	Let $D_{\Phi}(n,k)$ be the distribution over formulas $\phi$ where all clauses are chosen independently from $D_{replace}$ and the number of clauses is chosen from a Poisson distribution with mean $d_kn$.
prelims.tex:96:%	Let $D_{\Phi}'(n,k)$ be the distribution over formulas $\phi$ where all clauses are chosen independently from $D_{unique}$ and the number of clauses is chosen from a Poisson distribution with mean $d_kn$.
prelims.tex:100:Let $D_R(m,n,k)$ be the distribution over formulas $\phi$ where all $m$ clauses are chosen independently from  $D_{replace}$.
prelims.tex:104:%	Let $D_R'(m,n,k)$ be the distribution over formulas $\phi$ where all $m$ clauses are chosen independently from  $D_{unique}$.
prelims.tex:108:Let $D_S(m,n,k)$ be the uniform distribution over \emph{satisfied} formulas $\phi$ where all $m$ clauses are chosen from $D_{replace}$.
prelims.tex:112:Let $\DPc(n, k, \vec{a})$ (which we refer to as ``the planted-clause distribution'') be the uniform distribution over the $(2^k-1)n^k$ clauses $c$ which are satisfied by $\vec{a}$.
prelims.tex:116:Let $\DPa(m, n, k, \vec{a})$ (which we refer to as ``the planted distribution'') be the distribution over formulas $\phi$ where every clause is picked IID from $\DPc(n, k, \vec{a})$. Note that this is equivalent to the uniform distribution over formulas $\phi$ which are satisfied by $\vec{a}$ and where all $m$ clauses are in the support of $D_{replace}$.
prelims.tex:120:Let $U_{\vec{a}}(n)$ be the uniform distribution over assignments of length $n$, $\{0, 1\}^n$.
prelims.tex:124:Let $\ncs(\phi, \vec{v})$ be the number of clauses in $\phi$ satisfied by the assignment $\vec{v}$.
prelims.tex:128:Let $\ncus(\phi, \vec{v})$ be the number of clauses in $\phi$ left unsatisfied by the assignment $\vec{v}$.
prelims.tex:132:%	set $D_S'(m,n,k)$ be the uniform distribution over \emph{satisfied} formulas $\phi$ where all $m$ clauses are chosen from $D_{unique}$.
prelims.tex:137:%There are several different possible ways to formally define the problem of random $k$-CNF SAT, each of which has been used at some point in past work on this well-studied problem. We will briefly explain why our algorithm achieves good performance on all of these different variations of the problem; however, most of our analysis will focus on the first and most commonly-studied of the variations listed below, $D_{\Phi}(n,k)$.
prelims.tex:140:%    \item $\phi$ is drawn uniformly at random from $D_R(m,n,k)$.
prelims.tex:141:%    \item $\phi$ is drawn uniformly at random from $D_{\Phi}(n,k)$.
prelims.tex:142:%    \item $\phi$ is drawn uniformly at random from $D_{\Phi}'(n,k)$.
prelims.tex:143:%    \item $\phi$ is drawn uniformly at random from $D_{R}'(m,n,k)$.
prelims.tex:149:%We define \RandomSat to be the problem of Boolean satisfiability on an input chosen uniformly at random from a set of formulas $U_\phi$. Every $\phi \in U_\phi$ is a Boolean formula in CNF form, with each disjunctive clause containing exactly $k$ literals. Let $n$ be the number of variables and $m$ be the number of clauses in $\phi$. There are $mk$ total literals in $\phi$, with each literal in each clause chosen i.i.d. and uniformly at random from across the $2n$ different possible literals (two for each variable, one negated and one intact), with replacement. 
discussion.tex:4:Below we have three topics of discussion that did not fit in the main body of the paper. Subsection \ref{subsec:regularizeVStest} is a discussion of different ways to view the algorithmic framework of test and search. Readers who dislike the false positive and false negative framing may find this alternative analysis more appealing. Subsection \ref{subsec:motivation} further motivates our results. Subsection  \ref{subsec:kalphaVSexaustive} is primarily directed at readers who are interested in extending this work. 
discussion.tex:9:%We present a view of our algorithm's analysis where we view the algorithm as a test. 
discussion.tex:10:In our paper, we bound the false positive and true positive rates to show that our running time and correctness conditions are met. 
discussion.tex:12:However, given our analysis and algorithm, one could take a different approach to proving the soundness of our algorithm. Let $T$ be the threshold we use in \HammDist~for the number of clauses an assignment must satisfy to justify a local search in its neighborhood. This alternate approach focuses on individual satisfying assignments and their small-Hamming-distance neighborhoods.
discussion.tex:15:Call an assignment that satisfies a number of clauses above the  threshold, $T$, \emph{promising}. 
discussion.tex:17:Let the set of assignments within Hamming distance $\alpha$ of a given assignment $\vec{a}$ be $H(\vec{a}, \alpha, n)$. 
discussion.tex:19:Call an assignment, $\vec{a}$, a \emph{standard satisfying assignment} of a formula $\phi$ if both: (1) $\vec{a}$ satisfies the formula $\phi$ and (2) at least half of the assignments in $H(\vec{a}, \alpha, n)$ are \emph{promising}. 
discussion.tex:21:Call a formula \emph{stuffed} if the total number of \emph{promising} assignments is more than $4n^3 \cdot 2^n/\left( \binom{n}{\alpha n} k^{\alpha n}\right)$.
discussion.tex:23:Call a formula \emph{hollow} if the total number of \emph{promising} assignments at most $4n^3 \cdot 2^n/\left( \binom{n}{\alpha n} k^{\alpha n}\right)$.
discussion.tex:26:We know that $|H(\vec{a}, \alpha, n)| \geq \binom{n}{\alpha n}$. So, for any given standard satisfying assignment of the formula $\phi$, there are at least $\frac{1}{2} \cdot \binom{n}{\alpha n}$ assignments that are both promising and close enough in Hamming distance that when the local search is run, $\vec{a}$ will be found. 
discussion.tex:28:It follows that for any given standard satisfying assignment, $\vec{s}$, if we sample $n^2 \cdot 2^n/\binom{n}{\alpha n}$ assignments and run local searches on assignments that are promising, we will find $\vec{s}$ with high probability. 
discussion.tex:32:Now we give a proof sketch of our algorithm's correctness in terms of the new framework. 
discussion.tex:33:We want to prove that formulas $\phi$ are hollow with high probability when drawn from $D_{\Phi}(n,k)$. We additionally want to prove that, conditioned on a formula being satisfiable, there exists a standard satisfying assignment of the formula $\phi$ with high probability. 
discussion.tex:35:We will note that Lemma~\ref{lem:boundNumSamplesOurAlpha} does in fact prove that formulas drawn from $D_{\Phi}(n,k)$ are with high probability hollow. Furthermore, Corollary~\ref{cor:helpfulPlantedTo} proves that satisfied formulas drawn from $D_{\Phi}(n,k)$ have at least one standard satisfying assignment with high probability. 
discussion.tex:37:%We feel that this view of the algorithmic framework is more limiting than the one we used. Notably, a formula having a standard satisfying assignment is a stronger statement than a formula having a true positive rate of at least $\binom{n}{\alpha n}/\left(2 \cdot 2^n\right)$. The formulation of a standard satisfying assignment requires the true positives be clustered with a single satisfying assignment. We do prove this stronger statement, even though algorithmic correctness does not require it. 
discussion.tex:39:We present a more general framework in the main body of our paper, but some readers may prefer the framework presented here.% Those readers may prefer to think of the analyze our algorithm in terms of hollow formulas and standard satisfying assignments.
discussion.tex:44:We give some additional motivation for why our results are interesting, in particular our improved running time of $2^{n(1-\Omega(\lg^2(k))/k)}$. In `Tighter Hard Instances for PPSZ,' a distribution of hard examples for the PPSZ algorithm is produced \cite{harderExamplePPSZ}. On these instances PPSZ runs in time $2^{n(1-O(\lg(k)/k))}$. Given that both this lower bound on PPSZ and the algorithms of Vyas \cite{vyas2018super}~and Valiant \cite{Val} all run in $2^{n(1-O(\lg(k)/k))}$ time, one might believe that the best possible running time for random satisfiability is $2^{n(1-O(\lg(k)/k))}$ time. However, our algorithm, \HammDist, runs in time $2^{n(1-\Omega(\lg^2(k))/k)}$, which we consider remarkable. %There is an improvement over the previous work, above the lower bound on the running time of PPSZ. 
discussion.tex:46:%random satisfiability at the threshold is interesting and why our algorithmic improvement is interesting. 
discussion.tex:49:%Informally, it seems like the formulas that are hard to correctly identify as satisfiable or unsatisfiable should be clustered at the threshold. If this is true, then we would expect that algorithms run on these worst-case formulas should run in the same amount of time as algorithms run on most formulas at the threshold. If this informal understand is correct, then our algorithm implies that significant improvements to the worst case algorithms exist. 
discussion.tex:52:%Our improved running time of $2^{n(1-\Omega(\lg^2(k))/k)}$ time is  interesting.
discussion.tex:58:One question a skeptical reader might ask is: {\centering \emph{``How many satisfying assignments should I expect for satisfiable formulas at the threshold?''}} After all, if satisfiable formulas at the threshold have a very large number of satisfying assignments, then the problem of random satisfiability is easily solved. However, it is easy to show that the fraction of satisfying assignments at the threshold across all formulas is an exponentially small fraction of assignments. Specifically, the expected number of satisfying assignments in a randomly chosen formula, even conditioned on that formula being satisfied, is $O\left(2^{2n/e^{k/e^2}}\right)$. Note that $e^{k/e^2}$ grows exponentially faster than $\frac{k}{\lg^2(k)}$. 
discussion.tex:60:%An algorithm which randomly searches for these satisfying assignments, using only their extra density, will have a running time of, at best, $2^{n(1-O(1)/2^{k/e^2})}$. 
discussion.tex:62:So, although there may be many satisfying assignments at the threshold in an absolute sense, there are very few of them relative to the total number of assignments.
