discussion.tex:92:%In this section we propose conjectures for the running time of Random Satisfiability. These conjectures are primarily written to make clear where we as authors think the most important barriers lie. They are based on the worst-case Strong Exponential Time Hypothesis (SETH) \cite{CalabroIP09}.  
discussion.tex:94:%The authors of this paper disagree about the likelihood of these conjectures being true. However, we agree that improvements to Random SAT algorithms invalidating either conjecture would represent the introduction of some novel algorithmic technique or analysis tool that warrants attention.
discussion.tex:98:%First we define what we mean by solving the problem at the threshold. 
discussion.tex:100:%Say an algorithm $A$ ``solves'' the random $k$-satisfiability problem at the threshold if given a $\phi \sim D_{\Phi}(n,k)$:
discussion.tex:102:%	\item Conditioned on $\phi$ having a satisfying assignment, $A$ returns some satisfying assignment with high probability over both the randomized input and the randomness of the algorithm.
discussion.tex:103:%	\item Conditioned on $\phi$ having \emph{no} satisfying assignments, $A$ returns \textbf{False} with high probability over both the randomized input and the randomness of the algorithm.
discussion.tex:107:%We now propose a conjecture that no Random SETH algorithm can save a polynomial factor of $k$ in the exponent. We, unfortunately, have not been able to think of an informative name for this conjecture. Future work related to this conjecture includes attempting to violate the conjecture and improving its name. 
discussion.tex:110:%	Then, there is \emph{no} constant $\epsilon >0$ such that for all large enough constant $k$ some algorithm $A$ exists which solves the random $k$-satisfiability problem at the threshold in time 
discussion.tex:111:%	$$O(2^{n(1-1/k^{1-\epsilon})}).$$
discussion.tex:114:%Finally, we propose Random SETH (RSETH). Violating RSETH would give a clear separation between Random Satisfiability at the threshold and worst case satisfiability, at least for those who believe in SETH. 
