/tmp/tmp.ogMpAPtlDF/algorithm.tex:1:We will describe our algorithm for random $k$-SAT in this section.
/tmp/tmp.ogMpAPtlDF/algorithm.tex:3:Informally, our algorithm works as follows. Given an input formula, we will sample many randomly-chosen assignments. On those that have a high number of satisfied clauses, we will run the deterministic algorithm for finding a satisfying assignment given an assignment that is within a Hamming distance of at most $\alpha n$ of that satisfying assignment (i.e. a small-Hamming-distance assignment). %We describe our algorithm in a testing framework. 
/tmp/tmp.ogMpAPtlDF/algorithm.tex:5:Unsurprisingly, in the average case, small-Hamming-distance assignments satisfy more clauses than random assignments\footnote{Consider changing one variable's assignment at random; in this case, almost all clauses will remain satisfied. This phenomenon persists even when we flip several variables at once.}. In fact, for many choices of criterion there will be a discrepancy between the values achieved by small-Hamming-distance assignments and random assignments. Lemma \ref{lem:generalSmallHDf}, which characterizes this discrepancy, is general enough to be applied immediately to analyzing algorithms that make use of any clause-specific criterion. 
/tmp/tmp.ogMpAPtlDF/algorithm.tex:9:\begin{lemma}[Small Hamming Distance Search \cite{localSearchAlg}]
/tmp/tmp.ogMpAPtlDF/algorithm.tex:10:	There is a deterministic algorithm 
/tmp/tmp.ogMpAPtlDF/algorithm.tex:19:This algorithm simply takes the assignment $\vec{a}$ and branches on the first unsatisfied clause, trying all possible variable flips. For each assignment resulting from these possible variable flips, the algorithm repeats the process in what is now the first unsatisfied clause, until it either finds a satisfying assignment or has searched $\alpha n$ flips from the original assignment. This will deterministically yield a satisfying assignment, should one exist, within a Hamming distance of $\alpha n$ of the original assignment. 
/tmp/tmp.ogMpAPtlDF/algorithm.tex:21:So, if we find a small-Hamming-distance assignment and run \textsc{SAT-from-$\alpha$-Small-HD}($\phi,\vec{a}$) on this assignment, we are guaranteed to find the satisfying assignment. Therefore, we could randomly sample points until we expect to find an assignment at Hamming distance $\alpha n$ from the satisfying assignment (call this an $\alpha$-small-Hamming-distance assignment). This is indeed what Sch{\"{o}}ning's algorithm does for $\alpha = \Theta(1/k)$ \cite{randomLocalSearch}. 
/tmp/tmp.ogMpAPtlDF/algorithm.tex:23:A general class of improvements to this algorithm work by running \smlHDAlg($\phi,\vec{a}$) on only a cleverly-chosen subset of these sampled assignments. In our case, we choose this set to be assignments that satisfy an unusually large number of clauses, but in principle one could use any membership criterion for this set. 
/tmp/tmp.ogMpAPtlDF/algorithm.tex:27:We will have to draw samples until we would have found a satisfying assignment with high probability were one to exist. Next, we will have to run \smlHDAlg($\phi,\vec{a}$) at least once to find the satisfying assignment itself. Finally, we will have to run it once more for every false positive we find. Hence, the the generalized running time of this class of algorithms is 
/tmp/tmp.ogMpAPtlDF/algorithm.tex:34:This general formula is a powerful tool for analyzing the runtimes of algorithms from this class. For example, if we apply it to analyzing the algorithm of~\cite{localSearchAlg}, i.e. the special case where the test we use always returns a positive, we see that the third term in Equation~(\ref{eq:general_rt}) dominates, and that $p_{FP} \approx 1$ and $p_{TP} \approx \frac{\binom{n}{\alpha n}}{2^n}$, giving an overall expected runtime of $O^*(2^{n(1-\Theta(\frac{1}{k}))})$ when we choose $\alpha = \Theta(\frac{1}{k+1})$. In Appendix \ref{subsec:kalphaVSexaustive} we discuss a different deterministic search algorithm with a slightly improved runtime (yielding no relevant improvement on the runtime of the overall algorithm for our analysis). 
/tmp/tmp.ogMpAPtlDF/algorithm.tex:36:%We could also ask what would happen if we had a perfect or nearly-perfect polynomial-time test of whether or not an assignment was within Hamming distance $\alpha n$, but continued to restrict our attention to sampling algorithms, i.e. randomly choosing assignments and searching in the vicinity if the test returned a positive. In this case, we would find that the first and second terms in Equation~(\ref{eq:general_rt}) would dominate, and that $p_{FP} \approx 0$ and $p_{TP} \approx \binom{n}{\alpha n}/2^n$, giving an overall expected runtime of $O^*(2^{n(1-\Theta(\frac{\log \log k}{\log k}))})$ when we choose $\alpha = \Theta(\frac{1}{\log k})$. This ``best possible'' result gives us a sense for the limitations of this general approach; in particular, even with a polynomial-time test approaching perfect accuracy, no sample-and-search algorithm can run in faster than expected time $\Theta^*(2^n)$ in the limit of large $k$.
/tmp/tmp.ogMpAPtlDF/algorithm.tex:38:%Of course, in reality, we are hoping to analyze an algorithm with a test that is far from perfect; our analysis, however, still takes place within this framework. In the sections that follow, we bound the expected running time of our algorithm by bounding $p_{FP}$ and $p_{TP}$.
/tmp/tmp.ogMpAPtlDF/algorithm.tex:40:%So we have a test for each randomly sampled point that runs in polynomial time and has some false positive rate, $p_{FP}$, and some true positive rate $p_{TP}$. What is the running time of this algorithm? We must sample until we get an assignment that is a small hamming distance from the satisfying assignment if one exists. Then, on all assignments that tested positive we must run the algorithm to find the satisfying assignment. This gives us running time:
/tmp/tmp.ogMpAPtlDF/algorithm.tex:42:%So, fundamentally we will be bounding the false positive and true positive rates to prove our algorithm runs quickly and correctly. 
/tmp/tmp.ogMpAPtlDF/algorithm.tex:47:Our algorithm presents improvements for large $k$, but for small $k$ we will simply use the previous algorithm of Dantsin et al \cite{localSearchAlg}. 
/tmp/tmp.ogMpAPtlDF/algorithm.tex:48:\begin{lemma}[Algorithm for Small $k$ \cite{localSearchAlg}]
/tmp/tmp.ogMpAPtlDF/algorithm.tex:49:For $k \leq \kstr$ there exists a deterministic algorithm, \LocalSearch, that solves $k$-SAT in the worst case in time $2^{n(1-\gamma)}$ for some constant $\gamma>0$ \cite{localSearchAlg}.
/tmp/tmp.ogMpAPtlDF/algorithm.tex:52:We will now give pseudocode for the \HammDist~ algorithm in Algorithm \ref{alg:randomSatAlgo}. 
/tmp/tmp.ogMpAPtlDF/algorithm.tex:53:Let \textsc{NumClausesSAT}($\phi$,$\vec{a}$) return the number of clauses in $\phi$ satisfied by the assignment $\vec{a}$. In Appendix \ref{subsec:regularizeVStest}, we describe a different set of concepts with which the algorithm can be understood. 
