/tmp/tmp.ogMpAPtlDF/intro.tex:73:We also note that our algorithm can potentially be used as the seed for a worst-case algorithm. Informally, the correctness of the analysis in this paper depends only on the false positive and false negative rates being sufficiently low. As long as the inputs are guaranteed to come from a family of formulas for which this is the case, our algorithm will work even in the worst case. Or, to put it another way, to build a working worst-case algorithm using our algorithm as a template, one may now restrict one's attention to solving input formulas for which assignments in the neighborhood of the solution do \emph{not} have an abnormally-high number of satisfied clauses; our algorithm can solve the others.
