The paper’s first decisive warning is explicit: its planted distribution is easier than uniform random \(k\)-SAT, and planted recovery is polynomial once \(m=\Omega(n\log n)\). A viable Track A family must therefore stay at the paper’s threshold-scale \(m=\Theta(n)\), use growing search width \(n\), and empirically defeat literal-bias, local-search, and DPLL attacks; worst-case NP-hardness alone will not be enough.
