Rejected at Step 0; no compliant problem family exists under either track.

The naive planted-\(k\)-SAT family passes G and V, but the paper explicitly identifies that planted distribution as easier and provides no distributional-hardness theorem for it. Its main result is an exponential-time upper bound for uniform threshold formulas, not planted hardness. The cited planted-SAT literature also classifies this naive plant as first-order biased rather than “quiet.” [Paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.10618), [planted-SAT analysis](https://arxiv.org/abs/1311.4821).

The closest Track B alternative—Lemma 3.1’s bounded-Hamming correction task—also fails. On eight measured instances, the reference algorithm solved 8/8, requiring a median 30,118 literal inspections. The supposed compact route is the identical branching-and-rescanning algorithm, so there is no compression gap and G9(c)’s 300-operation cap is exceeded.

The complete, reviewable rationale and measurements are in [REJECTED.md](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1903.10618/REJECTED.md). Per the instructions, no generator, self-test, README, or oracle transcripts were manufactured after the decisive Step-0 rejection.
